.TH RUN-CHECKIN 1 "2026-10-12" "cti-agent 1.3" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
run-checkin \- fire one orchestrator heartbeat: Claude Code runs /checkin under a fixed tool allowlist
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B run\-checkin
.RB [ \-\-audit ]
.PP
.B sudo cti\-agent run\-checkin
.RB [ \-\-audit ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B run-checkin
runs one beat of the orchestrator: Claude Code, non-interactively, with the
prompt
.BR /checkin ,
in the orchestrator directory,
.I $FLEET_CODE/orchestrator
(\fI/opt/cti\-agent/orchestrator\fR on Linux), so that the installed
.I CLAUDE.md
and
.I .claude/skills/checkin
load. That directory is root-owned and holds only those files; the service
account can read it but not change it. On macOS, a developer checkout, the
beat starts in
.B FLEET_HOME
instead. The orchestrator does the judgment work (reading the board, noticing
a disabled timer, escalating); the timers do the guaranteed work.
.PP
With no argument it runs a beat. The one option,
.BR \-\-audit ,
runs the integrity and steering checks and stops. Any other argument is
refused (exit 2).
.PP
It is run by
.B cti\-agent\-checkin.service
from
.BR cti\-agent\-checkin.timer :
5 minutes after boot, then 2 hours after each beat, with up to 5 minutes'
random delay. The timer is deliberately not
.BR Persistent ,
so missed beats are not replayed after downtime. The unit runs as
.B ctiagent
in the
.B systemd\-journal
group (so the journal can be read), with
.BR UMask=0077 ,
a 30-minute start timeout, and
.B cti\-agent\-alert@.service
on failure.
.PP
The steps, in order:
.TP
.B 0. Code integrity
On Linux the runner refuses to start unless the code it will run cannot be
changed by the account running it. Each of
.IR run\-checkin ,
its directory,
.BR FLEET_CODE ,
.IR $FLEET_CODE/bin ,
and the granted binaries
.BR fleet\-board ,
.BR fleet\-db ,
.BR cti\-budget ,
.BR cti\-kev ,
.BR cti\-patchtuesday ,
.BR cti\-mailbox
and
.B cti\-alert
(those that exist; in practice every entry of
.IR $FLEET_CODE/bin ,
including
.BR fleet\-journal ),
the settings file
.IR $FLEET_CODE/checkin\-settings.json ,
and the orchestrator directory with its
.IR CLAUDE.md ,
.I .claude
and skills, must be owned by root, not writable by group or others, and
not writable by the running account. Symbolic links are judged by their
target. Otherwise the runner prints
.B REFUSING to start
and the offending path to standard error and exits 1.
It posts nothing to the board, because
.B fleet\-board
is one of the files being checked, and it does not start Claude Code; the
unit's
.B OnFailure
alert tells the operator. The reason is that the beat reads attacker-supplied
text and its allowlist is built here: if the service account could rewrite the
runner or a granted binary, a hostile advisory could change what every later
run executes with the fleet's credentials.
.B install\-fedora.sh
installs the code root-owned
.B 0755
under
.IR /opt/cti\-agent ,
which passes. The retired generic layout, with the code owned by the service
account, does not. The runner also refuses (exit 1) when
.IR CLAUDE.md ,
the
.I checkin
.I SKILL.md
or the settings file is missing, which is what a box upgraded without
re-running the installer looks like. On macOS the check is skipped with a log
line, since that is a developer's own checkout.
.TP
.B 0a. Steering audit
Claude Code loads more than it is told to: instructions, skills, commands,
agents, hooks, settings and MCP servers from its working directory and from
the account's home. The beat starts in a directory the account cannot write,
but the account's own
.B HOME
(\fI/var/lib/cti\-agent\fR) is writable by it, so a beat steered once could
leave something there to steer every later one. The runner therefore refuses
to start (exit 1, ERROR on the board) if any of these exists, since nothing
in this fleet installs them:
in the orchestrator directory,
.IR .claude/settings.json ,
.IR .claude/settings.local.json ,
.IR .mcp.json ,
.IR CLAUDE.local.md ,
and
.IR .claude/commands ,
.IR agents ,
.I hooks
or
.IR rules ;
in
.B HOME
or
.B CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
(default
.IR $HOME/.claude ),
.IR CLAUDE.md ,
.IR skills ,
.IR commands ,
.IR agents ,
.IR hooks ,
.IR rules ,
.IR output\-styles ,
.IR settings.local.json ,
.IR $HOME/CLAUDE.md ,
.IR $HOME/CLAUDE.local.md
and
.IR $HOME/.mcp.json .
It also refuses if
.I settings.json
in the config directory, or
.I ~/.claude.json
(Claude Code's own state file, which exists after the first run), sets hooks,
permissions, MCP servers, env, helper commands (API key, AWS, OTEL, status
line), plugins, or, per project, an
.B allowedTools
list. Other keys are ignored. The message names each file or key and says to
move it out of the account's reach; the installer does that on an existing
box, into
.IR /etc/cti\-agent/quarantine/ .
On macOS the audit is reported, not enforced.
.TP
.B 1. Find claude
.B CLAUDE_BIN
if set, which must be executable. Otherwise, on Linux, the first executable
of
.I /usr/bin/claude
and
.IR /usr/local/bin/claude ;
the binary found must pass the same root-owned check as the code, or the
beat refuses to start. On macOS, the first executable of
.IR $HOME/.local/bin/claude ,
.IR /usr/bin/claude ,
.IR /usr/local/bin/claude ,
.IR /opt/homebrew/bin/claude ,
.IR /snap/bin/claude ,
then
.B claude
on
.BR PATH .
Not found: an ERROR board line and exit 1.
.TP
.B 2. Credentials
Counts which of
.BR ANTHROPIC_API_KEY ,
.B ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN
and
.B CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN
are non-empty. More than one is refused (exit 1), because Claude Code would
pick one and it may bill a different account from the one intended. None is
accepted only if a stored login exists: a non-empty
.I .credentials.json
in
.B CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
(default
.IR $HOME/.claude )
on Linux, or the
.B "Claude Code\-credentials"
Keychain item on macOS. Otherwise exit 1. Each refusal posts an ERROR board
line. The journal records which credential is in use, by name only, never
any part of its value.
.TP
.B 3. Lock
The directory
.I $FLEET_HOME/.checkin.lock
is created. If it already exists and is under 30 minutes old, the previous
beat is still running: the runner logs it and exits 0. An older lock is
taken over. The lock is removed on exit.
.TP
.B 4. Budget gate
If
.I $FLEET_CODE/bin/cti\-budget
is executable, runs
.BR "cti\-budget check \-\-print\-alert\-flag" ,
which holds the rolling-window ceiling, the daily ceiling and the cooldown
after a rate limit.
Exit 3 means hold: the reason is logged and posted to the board as INFO;
if the last output line is
.B alert
(the first beat of a distinct denial) and
.I cti\-alert
is executable,
.B cti\-alert \-\-unit cti\-agent\-checkin.service \-\-kind HOLD
mails the operator; then exit 0, because a working brake is not a failed
unit. Any other non-zero status is logged and the beat proceeds (the gate
fails open). If
.B cti\-budget
is absent there is no gate.
.TP
.B 5. Beat
.EX
claude \-p /checkin \-\-permission\-mode default \e
       \-\-setting\-sources project \e
       \-\-settings $FLEET_CODE/checkin\-settings.json \e
       \-\-strict\-mcp\-config \e
       \-\-allowedTools "$ALLOWED_TOOLS" \e
       \-\-disallowedTools "Edit,Write,NotebookEdit" </dev/null
.EE
.IP
stdin is closed, so nothing can be approved interactively.
.B \-\-setting\-sources project
loads no settings from the user's config directory, and
.B \-\-strict\-mcp\-config
with none given loads no MCP server. The root-owned settings file turns hooks
off
.RB ( disableAllHooks ),
stops project MCP servers being enabled, disables bypass-permissions mode and
denies
.BR Edit ,
.BR Write ,
.BR NotebookEdit ,
.B WebFetch
and
.BR WebSearch .
The settings flags are omitted only on macOS when the file is absent. Output goes to
the journal and to a temporary file. The allowlist and the disallowed tools
are printed to the journal before the beat.
.TP
.B 6. Record
If
.B cti\-budget
is present,
.B cti\-budget record \-\-exit
.I rc
.B \-\-output
.I last\-4000\-bytes
.B \-\-note checkin
counts the beat against the ceilings and classifies its output. Exit 4 from
the record means the account is out of credit: a HOLD, posted to the board
and sent through
.BR cti\-alert ,
and the runner exits 0. Any other record result is ignored and the runner
exits with Claude Code's status.
.PP
A non-zero exit from
.B claude
in step 5 does not end the runner: errexit is suspended around that one
command, so step 6 always runs, every beat is recorded with
.BR cti\-budget ,
and an out-of-credit failure reaches the exit-4 HOLD. (Before 1.3 it ended
the runner at once, so failed beats were never counted against the
ceilings or backed off.)
.PP
Everything in
.I fleet.env
is exported into Claude Code's environment, Graph and scanner credentials
included.
.SS What the orchestrator may run
The beat has no file-edit tools:
.BR Edit ,
.B Write
and
.B NotebookEdit
are disallowed, because nothing in
.B /checkin
writes a file (memory, tasks and board posts go through the commands below)
and an edit grant would let a beat steered by hostile input rewrite its own
instructions. Read-only file tools remain, limited to
.IR $FLEET_HOME/reports ,
.I logs
and
.I archive
(a
.BI Read( path /**)
grant each; it covers Glob and Grep). The beat does not start in
.BR FLEET_HOME ,
so nothing else there is readable, and
.I state/
is not: memory goes through
.BR fleet\-db .
Every shell command needs a grant, and with no terminal anything not granted
is denied. The grant is built in the runner,
not in a settings file the agent could edit, from absolute paths under
.I $FLEET_CODE/bin
(shown here as
.IR bin/ ).
Each entry is a prefix: the command as written, then any further arguments.
.TP
.B bin/fleet\-board
Any arguments.
.TP
.B bin/fleet\-db
Only the subcommands
.BR recent ,
.BR findings ,
.BR was\-sent ,
.BR rollup ,
.BR remember ,
.BR task ,
.BR mail ,
.B ack
and
.BR vacuum .
Not
.B sent
(it would mark a digest delivered and stop that day's real send),
.B finding
(it rewrites what the digest reports),
.B note
or
.BR init .
.TP
.B bin/cti\-budget
Any arguments, including
.B record
and
.BR status .
.TP
.B bin/cti\-kev
Any arguments.
.TP
.B bin/cti\-patchtuesday \-\-dry\-run
Only with
.B \-\-dry\-run
as the first argument. That excludes
.BR \-\-mark\-sent ,
which the binary refuses alongside
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
and so do the flags that would make a dry run fetch from a chosen host or
read a chosen folder:
.BR \-\-url\-msrc ,
.BR \-\-url\-bleeping ,
.B \-\-url\-qualys
and
.BR \-\-drop\-dir .
.TP
.B bin/cti\-mailbox
Exactly that, with no arguments: the dry run. Not
.BR \-\-for\-real .
.TP
.B systemctl list\-timers
Any further arguments; the prefix cannot become stop or disable.
.TP
.B bin/cti\-alert
Any arguments. The only mail the orchestrator can cause: there is no
recipient flag, so it reaches
.B FLEET_OPERATOR_EMAIL
and nobody else.
.TP
.B bin/fleet\-journal
Only the options
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-\-since ,
.B \-n
and
.BR \-\-no\-pager ,
with units limited to
.BR cti\-agent\-* .
This replaces a grant of
.B journalctl
itself, whose
.B \-\-cursor\-file
writes to any path as the service account, which could reset
.I state/budget.json
(the quota) or overwrite
.IR state/memory.db .
See
.BR fleet\-journal (1).
.PP
Nothing else is granted: not
.B cti\-mailer
or
.BR mailer.py ,
not any
.B run\-*
runner, not
.BR cti\-jira ,
.B cti\-appscan
or
.BR cti\-domains ,
not plain
.BR journalctl ,
and no general shell.
.B systemctl
is matched by bare name, not by path.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-audit
Run the integrity check (step 0) and the steering audit (step 0a), print the
result, and exit 0 if both pass or 1 if either fails, without taking the lock,
checking the budget or starting a beat. Nothing is posted to the board on
success. The installer runs it as the service account at the end of an
install; run it yourself after moving files around:
.B "sudo cti\-agent run\-checkin \-\-audit".
.PP
Any other argument prints a usage line and exits 2.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.I fleet.env
is sourced with every assignment exported, and its values replace the
process environment, except
.BR FLEET_HOME ,
.B FLEET_CODE
and
.BR FLEET_FEEDS ,
which keep the value the unit or wrapper injected.
.TP
.BR FLEET_HOME ", " FLEET_CODE ", " FLEET_ENV ", " FLEET_FEEDS
Fleet layout, as for
.BR run\-digest (1).
.B FLEET_HOME
defaults to
.IR $HOME/fleet ,
.B FLEET_CODE
to
.BR $FLEET_HOME ,
.B FLEET_ENV
to
.IR $FLEET_HOME/fleet.env .
.TP
.B HOME
Where the default Claude Code locations are searched;
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent
under the unit and the wrapper.
.TP
.B CLAUDE_BIN
Absolute path to
.BR claude ;
empty searches (section 7).
.TP
.BR ANTHROPIC_API_KEY ", " ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN ", " CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN
The credential. At most one may be set (section 7).
.TP
.B CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
Where a stored login is looked for.
Default:\~\fI$HOME/.claude\fR (section 7).
.TP
.BR FLEET_BUDGET_WINDOW_HOURS ", " FLEET_BUDGET_WINDOW_BEATS ", " FLEET_BUDGET_DAILY_BEATS ", " FLEET_BUDGET_BACKOFF_BASE_HOURS ", " FLEET_BUDGET_BACKOFF_MAX_HOURS ", " FLEET_BUDGET_FILE
The ceilings, read by
.B cti\-budget
(section 8). Defaults: 4 beats per 5 hours, 14 a day, backoff from 0.5 to
6 hours.
.TP
.B FLEET_OPERATOR_EMAIL
Where
.B cti\-alert
sends a HOLD (section 4).
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Directory for the beat's output copy.
Default:\~\fI/tmp\fR (private to the unit).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent/.checkin.lock
The overlap lock, a directory. A lock over 30 minutes old is taken over.
.TP
.I /opt/cti\-agent/orchestrator/CLAUDE.md
The orchestrator's standing instructions, installed root-owned by the
installer. (Before 1.4, in
.IR /var/lib/cti\-agent ;
the installer moves the old copy to
.IR /etc/cti\-agent/quarantine/ .)
.TP
.I /opt/cti\-agent/orchestrator/.claude/skills/checkin/SKILL.md
The
.B /checkin
skill, installed root-owned by the installer.
.TP
.I /opt/cti\-agent/checkin\-settings.json
The settings file passed with
.BR \-\-settings ,
installed root-owned
.BR 0644 .
.TP
.I /opt/cti\-agent/.orchestrator\-CLAUDE.md.sha256
The installer's record of the
.I CLAUDE.md
it last installed, used to notice operator edits.
.TP
.I /etc/cti\-agent/quarantine/TIMESTAMP/
Where the installer puts the old instructions, skills and any planted Claude
Code files it finds in the service account's reach. Root-only; nothing is
deleted.
.TP
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent/.claude/.credentials.json
A stored Claude Code login, when no credential variable is set.
.TP
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent/budget.json
The
.B cti\-budget
ledger, unless
.B FLEET_BUDGET_FILE
names another.
.TP
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent/board.md
The fleet board the runner and the orchestrator post to.
.TP
.I $TMPDIR/cti\-checkin.XXXXXX
The beat's output, passed to
.B cti\-budget record
and removed on exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The beat ran and Claude Code exited 0; or it was skipped because the
previous beat holds the lock; or
.B cti\-budget
held it (exit 3 from the check); or the record reported the account out of
credit (exit 4). The last two post to the board and may alert, but are not
unit failures.
.TP
.B 1
The code integrity check failed (step 0), files are missing, or the steering
audit found files that could steer the beat (step 0a), so nothing was started
(also the result of a failed
.BR \-\-audit );
.B claude
was not found,
.B CLAUDE_BIN
is not executable, more than one credential is set, or none is set and no
stored login exists.
.TP
.B 2
An argument other than
.BR \-\-audit .
.TP
.I other
Claude Code's own exit status.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check the beat's code and instructions without starting one:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent run\-checkin \-\-audit
.EE
.PP
Run one beat now and watch it:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent run\-checkin
.EE
.PP
See how much of the budget is left, and why a beat was held:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent cti\-budget status
journalctl \-u cti\-agent\-checkin.service \-n 100
.EE
.PP
Check when the next beat is due:
.PP
.EX
systemctl list\-timers cti\-agent\-checkin.timer
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cti\-budget (1),
.BR cti\-alert (1),
.BR fleet\-board (1),
.BR fleet\-db (1),
.BR fleet\-journal (1),
.BR cti\-kev (1),
.BR cti\-patchtuesday (1),
.BR cti\-mailbox (1),
.BR cti\-agent (1),
.BR claude (1)
.PP
